WEBVTT

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.067
Hi, my name is Selina.

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:05.238
I'm a third year studying Electrical
and Computer Engineering at Carnegie Mellon University.

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:09.943
One of the classes I took in my
first year was Acting for Non-Majors.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:16.516
So this is a Gen Ed course, which
is one of the requirements in CIT, my college,

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:19.919
where you have to take courses that
are more closely related to humanities

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.121
opposed to technicals.

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:26.092
And this course helped me really
wind down after technical courses.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:30.096
It was a really good change of pace and
I was able to meet a lot of new people

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:34.467
in different majors opposed to
the typical engineering majors, math majors

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.502
that I see in my courses.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.270
It was held in the School of Drama,

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:43.209
and one of our requisites of this course
was to attend a CMU drama play.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:46.713
So CMU School of Purnell,
which is our drama school,

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:52.218
hosts a lot of plays that are
directed and starred by CMU students.

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:56.589
And it's free for CMU students
so long as you can grab the tickets in time.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:01.061
Our course required us to watch
a play called Arcadia by Tom Stoppard,

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:03.163
and it was directed by Kyle Haden.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.134
So this play featured themes of love
versus intellect, order versus entropy,

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:11.971
the nature of time and truth
across 1809 and the present day.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.406
It was a three-hour play

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:18.645
and I was able to get all my friends
to join and watch our first CMU play together.

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.413
The actors were really fantastic,

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.382
even though they are still students.

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:25.285
Some of them were playing two characters
and you could really see

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:28.955
a stark difference in the mannerism and
behaviors of these characters.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.258
I think being able to
watch CMU drama plays,

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.561
which is entirely conducted by students,

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:38.998
is a great way for us to learn
more about the different colleges

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:44.504
across our university and be able
to interact with other students' works

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:48.975
and also be able to just learn something
outside of what we are taught in school.